# H15h — the four H15g review items, one number grammar, and the grammar table

Worktree `D:\Projects\Camel-wt\h15h-cast`, branch `handover/H15h-cast-text` (from `develop`).
Everything is left in the working tree: no commit, no new branch, no push.

This is H15h, the four follow-ups of the H15g review:

1. a text field inside a struct literal reports the type mismatch at that field;
2. a cast to the value's own type does nothing and is legal;
3. `let x: string = 42` is the mirror of H15g's `let x: int = '42'`: a type mismatch that points
   at `string(42)`;
4. one number grammar for entry arguments and source literals, read by the same parse function,
   checked in both directions against what the source really accepts.

The design frame is design principles **5.1** (a literal takes its type from the position it is
written in; a value is never wrapped or cut to fit a type; there is no implicit conversion, in
either direction, between a text and a value), **5.2** (an empty literal takes its type from its
position), **10.6** (a value that cannot be read is a located error that says what is wrong) and
language reference **2.5** (the number tokens), with H15f's rule that every refusal uses a
catalogue sentence.

A fifth, small item arrived while this work was in progress: the editor grammar's
`builtin-types` rule was missing `c64` and `c128`, so `feat/tools/grammar.plan.toml`'s
`grammar.tables` failed. It is included in §5.

---

## 1. Item 1 — a text field inside a struct literal

### 1.1 What was wrong

The GCT built a composite literal into a `data_ptr_t` and then converted the **whole** value to
the declared type. A text field made the conversion fail, and the failure was reported for the
literal as a whole: `"This value has type 'string', …"` at the declaration, or worse a message
about the literal not being representable. In GIR, `elementEntersIn` only let a numeric element
enter a position (`literalElementInType` returned `nullptr` for a `string`), so a text element of
a struct argument made the call a `NoMatchingFunction` instead of naming the field.

H27c had already recorded an array element's origin (`Tree::LiteralElement`) and reported an
element whose value did not fit at the element. Struct and tuple literals did not record their
fields' positions, so the same report was impossible for them.

### 1.2 What changed

| place | change |
|---|---|
| `src/compile/gct/builder.cpp`, `Builder::visitStructInto`, `visitStructData`, `visitTupleData` | each field/element records its position (and exact value, where it has one) with `Tree::LiteralElement`, as `visitArrayInto` already did (H27c). A `StructData` stores its fields in **name order**, so the recorded elements are reordered to that order before `Tree::setLiteralElements`: the record is indexed by the data's field slot, not by the order the source wrote the fields. This also fixes a latent H27c alignment bug for numeric fields written out of name order. |
| `src/compile/gct/builder.cpp`, new `Builder::rejectTextInLiteral` | walks a composite literal against the type of its position. A `string` where the position expects another type reports `SemanticDiag::DeclaredTypeMismatch` at the **element's** origin and commits the element's type (`string`) and the position's type (`float`). It recurses through `Struct`, `Tuple` and `Array`; it returns `false` when there is no such element, and the caller keeps its old report. |
| `src/compile/gct/builder.cpp`, `Builder::declaredValue` | calls `rejectTextInLiteral` for a composite literal before `convertTo`. A **lone** text value (`let x: int = '42'`) is not routed through it, so H15g's position (the declaration) is unchanged; see §3. |
| `src/compile/gir/builder_control.cpp`, `elementEntersIn` | a `string` element now enters its position's type (it is the element's own mismatch), instead of the whole literal being another type. |
| `src/compile/gir/builder_control.cpp`, `literalElementInType` | `string` is reported by `rejectLiteralData` at the element's origin before the numeric-literal checks; the check for a written value and numeric types follows. This also covers a text element of an array argument, which now reports at the element instead of leaving the call unmatched. |
| `src/compile/gir/builder_diagnostics.cpp`, `rejectLiteralData` | the mismatch branch now covers `data string || type string` and commits the value's own type, so the same function serves both directions (§3). |
| `src/core/data/composite/tuple.cpp`, `TupleData::convertTo` | an element that is null, or whose conversion fails, makes the whole conversion return `nullptr` instead of building a tuple with a hole. Before, `let t: (float, float) = ('1.5', 2.0)` reached a null element and crashed with an access violation (exit `-1073741819`); the field is now reported by the path above. |

The result is one rule in both builders: the position that cannot take the value reports the
mismatch, at the position where the value is written. Examples (interpreter, `-E short`):

```text
context_struct_field_text.cml:13:33: [Error]: This value has type 'string', which is not the declared type 'float'.
context_tuple_field_text.cml:8:30: [Error]: This value has type 'string', which is not the declared type 'float'.
context_nested_field_text.cml:11:33: [Error]: This value has type 'string', which is not the declared type 'float'.
context_argument_field_text.cml:14:29: [Error]: This value has type 'string', which is not the declared type 'float'.
context_return_field_text.cml:9:34: [Error]: This value has type 'string', which is not the declared type 'float'.
```

Not a `LiteralNotRepresentable`, and not "the text is not a number": a text and a number are
different types (5.1).

### 1.3 Shapes locked

`test/plans/feat/compile/literal_context.plan.toml`:

| test | shape |
|---|---|
| `context.struct_field_text` | named struct literal, fields written **out of name order** (`{ y: 2.0, x: '1.5' }`), so the position follows the field, not its data slot |
| `context.tuple_field_text` | positional tuple literal |
| `context.nested_field_text` | field of a struct literal inside another struct literal |
| `context.argument_field_text` | field of a struct literal argument, fields out of name order |
| `context.return_field_text` | field of a returned struct literal |

Each expects `DeclaredTypeMismatch` at the exact element line/column with the two type names, and
explicitly expects neither `LiteralNotRepresentable` nor "the text is not a number"; the argument
