**Remaining gap, out of this item's scope:** a **tuple** literal argument written as
`f(('1.5', 2.0))` still reports `NoMatchingFunction`, because the GIR's
`compositeDataInType` has no `Tuple` branch (tuple arguments were never entered into a
parameter's tuple type; the H15g tuple conversion only covered declarations and returns). The
declaration, nested-field and return tuple shapes are covered. This is recorded in §7, not fixed
here: adding tuple argument entering would change overload resolution, which is a separate
change.

## 2. Item 2 — a cast to the value's own type

`Type::castSafetyFrom` starts with `if (equals(sourceType)) return CastSafety::Safe;`
(`src/core/type/base.cpp`). The composite types already answered this themselves (H15c/H15g);
the equality test covers the types that live in a slot: the primitives, `string`, the complex
numbers and any other type without fields. Converting a value to its own type is therefore legal
and does nothing, as design principles 5.1 says, whether the value is a literal or computed, and
whether the type is written directly or through an alias.

A real conversion is still refused: equality is false, and the existing rules apply (for example
`'1' as int` remains `DynamicCastForbidden`, locked by H15g's `context.text_as_int`).

`test/cases/compile/casts/same_type.cml` and its four plan tests were extended from arrays,
tuples and structs to every kind:

```camel
println('x' as string)          // the text literal itself
let text: string = 'y'
println(text as string)         // a computed text
println(n as int)               // a computed int
println(b as bool)
println(f as float)
println(z as c128)
println(w as c64)
println(3 as int)               // the literal path
println(1.5 as float)
```

All four back ends agree on the output (`same_type.nvm/fvm/jit/prl`), and the case still checks
the alias and literal-composite forms it had.

## 3. Item 3 — a number where a text is declared

### 3.1 The rule

There is no implicit conversion from a value to a text either (5.1). The GCT's
`reportLiteralCastFailure` used to decide "type mismatch" only when the *source* was a `string`;
it now decides it when either side is a `string`:

```cpp
if (data->type()->code() == TypeCode::String || type->code() == TypeCode::String) { … }
```

and commits the value's own type (`int`, `float`, `bool`, `c128`, `int[]`, …) and the declared
type (`string`). An explicit cast (`42 as string`) is still `DynamicCastForbidden`, exactly
mirroring H15g's `'42' as int`. The GIR's `rejectLiteralData` uses the same symmetric condition,
so a value whose type is only known after the GCT (a variable, a call result) reports the same
diagnostic.

The `DeclaredTypeMismatch` help gained the missing sentence: it now names `string(n)` as the
conversion that writes a number as text, beside `float(n)`, `int(s)` / `parse_int(s)` and
`v as T` (`src/core/error/diagnostics/messages/semantic.cpp`; `docs/diagnostics.md`
regenerated).

### 3.2 Neighbours

`let x: string = <value>` reports with the value's own type for every kind checked:

| value | reported type |
|---|---|
| `42` | `int` |
| `1.5` | `float` |
| `true` | `bool` |
| `1+2i` | `c128` |
| `[1, 2]` | `int[]` |
| `{ x: 1 }` | `{ x: int }` |
| `()` | `void` |
| `[('a', 1)] as Map<string, int>` | `Map<string, int>` |

All are `DeclaredTypeMismatch` at the declaration, with `string` as the declared type and no
`LiteralNotRepresentable` / "cannot be represented" wording. The last row is a computed value,
so it exercises the GIR branch rather than the GCT literal branch.

Locked by `context.declared_string_int`, `context.declared_string_array`,
`context.declared_string_complex` in `literal_context.plan.toml`; the int case also checks the
new help sentence. The float, bool, unit, struct and map neighbours were checked by hand with
the same result.

## 4. Item 4 — one number grammar for entry arguments and source literals

### 4.1 One parser

A new `camel::utils::parseNumberToken<T>` (`include/camel/utils/number_text.h`) reads one whole
number token of the language: an optional sign, then an integer token (decimal, `0x` hex, `0b`
binary, or octal with a leading `0`) or a real token (digits on both sides of a point and/or an
exponent), and nothing else. It is now the parser at every place a number is written as text:

| place | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| a source literal, GCT `visitLiteral` (`Integer`, `Real`) | `parseIntegerLiteral` / `parseNumber<double>` | `parseNumberToken<int64_t>` / `parseNumberToken<double>` |
| `--arg` of `int` / `i32` / `float` / `f32`, interpreter (`src/execute/entry_args.cpp`) | `parseWholeInteger` / `parseWholeFloat` | `parseNumberToken` |
| each part of `c64` / `c128`, interpreter (`complexFromText`) | `parseWholeFloat` | `parseNumberToken` |
| the same in a `std::cpp` program (`include/camel/standalone/entry.h`) | `parseWholeInteger` / `parseWholeFloat` | `parseNumberToken` |

`parseWholeInteger` / `parseWholeFloat` remain for the **conversion functions**
(`int(s)`, `float(s)`, `parse_int(s)`, `parse_float(s)`), whose text rule is deliberately the
decimal-only one of H15f (including refusing an underflow as out of range). They are not the
entry reader any more.

`parseNumberToken` details that the two sides now share:

- an **integer token is tried first**, so `077` is octal 63 (not decimal 77) and `0xFF` is 255,
  including in a float position (a source literal in a float position is that integer converted
  to the float's width);
- an integer token whose value does not fit an `int64` is `OutOfRangeInteger`, reported with the
  integer's range — not the float's, which would be a false statement about the text;
- a **real token** must have digits on both sides of a point and/or an exponent; `.5`, `5.` and
  a hexadecimal float are not tokens;
- the lexer's ambiguous leading-zero runs with an exponent (`077e2`) are not real tokens; a
  leading-zero multi-digit run without a point is refused even when it has an exponent;
- a real token too small to tell from zero is **zero**, as a source literal is (`1e-9999`);
  one too large is out of range.

A width bug found while locking this: `realValue` distinguished overflow from underflow after a
`from_chars` `result_out_of_range` with a **`double`** C parse, so `3.5e38` looked finite and was
read as an underflow (zero) for an `f32` parameter, and `3.5e38+1i` became `0+1i`. The
distinguishing parse is now at `T`'s own width (`strtof` for `float`, `strtod` for `double`), as
the libc++ fallback already was; `entry_args.f32_overflow_refused`,
`complex_c64_real_part_out_of_range` and `complex_c64_imaginary_part_out_of_range` are green
again.

### 4.2 What the source actually accepts, and what the entry reader accepts

The review's working assumption was that `0x1.8p1`, `.5` and `5.` are valid source literals.
They are not. The table is what the compiler does, not what the review assumed:

| form | source literal | `--arg` | conversion text (`float(s)` / `int(s)`) |
|---|---|---|---|
| `42`, `-42`, `+42` | yes | yes | yes |
| `0x10`, `0b1010`, `077` (= 63) | yes | yes | **no** (`int('0x10')` is invalid; `int('077')` is decimal 77 — the conversion text rule is decimal digits only, unchanged) |
| `0xFF` in a `float` / `f32` position | yes (255.0) | yes | no |
| `1.5`, `1e3`, `1E3`, `+1.5` | yes | yes | yes |
| `1e-9999` | yes (0.0) | yes (0.0) | **no**, `ConversionOutOfRange` (H15f: underflow refused) |
| `1e9999` | no, `LiteralNotRepresentable` (float range) | no, out of range (float range) | no, out of range |
| an integer token over `int64` (`0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF`), in a float position too | no, `LiteralNotRepresentable` (int range) | no, out of range (integer range) | no |
| `0x1.8p1` | no (lexes `0x1` then `.8p1`; the `.` is index access, `TypeNotIndexable`) | no, not a number | no |
| `.5` | no, syntax error | no, not a number | no (H15e tightened this) |
| `5.` | no, syntax error | no, not a number | no |
| `09` | not one token: the lexer cuts it into `0` and `9`; `let x: int = 09` compiles as the value `0` and a stray expression statement `9` | no, not a number (the whole text must be one token) | yes, decimal 9 (`int('09')`; the conversion text is decimal digits only) |
| `1e3` where an `int` is declared | no, `LiteralNotRepresentable` ("the value would be truncated") | no, not a number | no |
| `077e2`, `0xFFe-3`, `0b1e5` | no, `InvalidLiteral` (an exponent is decimal only) | no, not a number | `float('077e2')` is 7700.0 (decimal digits; leading zeros allowed); the other two are invalid |
| `inf`, `-inf`, `nan` | no (an unresolved reference: `inf` is not a literal token) | **yes**, the printed forms of a float (H15e) | yes |

Three findings are worth stating plainly:

1. **The review's premise about hexadecimal floats and the two dot forms is wrong.** The source
   refuses `0x1.8p1`, `.5` and `5.`, and the language reference 2.5.1/2.5.2 says the same
   (hexadecimal has no float form; a point needs digits on both sides). No reference change was
   made; the parser is the one that is right, and `entry_args.float_hex_refused`,
   `float_trailing_dot_refused`, `float_leading_dot_refused` already recorded it.
2. **`09` is a lexer cut, not a number token.** `09` is `0` then `9` by the lexer's documented
   longest-match rule (the same rule that makes `089` two tokens), and the parser accepts a
   second expression statement, so `let x: int = 09; println(x)` prints `0`. That is not the
   literal grammar accepting `09`; the entry reader requires the whole argument to be one token
   and rightly refuses it. This is recorded as an observation, not changed (§7).
3. **`inf` / `nan` are conversion-text forms, not literal tokens.** They stay accepted by the
   entry reader (H15d/H15e: reference 2.5.2's written forms of a float), while a source program
   cannot write `inf` as a literal (it is an unresolved name). `parseNumberToken` reads them
   because the entry reader uses it; the GCT only ever calls it on lexer number tokens, so the
   source cannot reach that branch. This is the one deliberate difference between "a number
   token" and "the text of a number" and is left as an open question (§7).

